  "description": "Symmetrically zero-pad a grayscale image border and extract a centered spatial crop of designated dimensions.\n\nPadding followed by random or centered cropping is standard preprocessing in vision pipelines to introduce translation invariance.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nI_{\\text{pad}} = \\operatorname{Pad}(I, p), \\quad I_{\\text{crop}} = I_{\\text{pad}}[h_{\\text{start}} : h_{\\text{start}}+H_c, \\, w_{\\text{start}} : w_{\\text{start}}+W_c]\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Apply symmetric constant zero padding of size p to all borders\n2. Compute center bounding coordinates for target dimensions (crop_h, crop_w)\n3. Slice and return centered image patch\n\n### Returns\nNested list representing cropped 2D array.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Zero Pad and Center Crop\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nImages have unique spatial properties: neighboring pixels are strongly correlated, and visual patterns (like edges, corners, or textures) appear anywhere in an image. Computer vision algorithms use spatial inductive biases like translation equivariance and local receptive fields to process image grids efficiently. **Zero Pad and Center Crop** operates directly on spatial coordinates or bounding box geometries.\n\nPadding followed by random or centered cropping is standard preprocessing in vision pipelines to introduce translation invariance. In this problem, the goal is to: symmetrically zero-pad a grayscale image border and extract a centered spatial crop of designated dimensions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nI_{\\text{pad}} = \\operatorname{Pad}(I, p), \\quad I_{\\text{crop}} = I_{\\text{pad}}[h_{\\text{start}} : h_{\\text{start}}+H_c, \\, w_{\\text{start}} : w_{\\text{start}}+W_c]\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Apply symmetric constant zero padding of size p to all borders\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute center bounding coordinates for target dimensions (crop_h, crop_w)\n3. **Phase 3**: Slice and return centered image patch\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Spatial Locality**: Sliding spatial filters across multi-channel feature maps aggregates contextual clues across local neighborhoods.\n* **Object Detection Pipelines**: Bounding box metrics (such as IoU and Non-Maximum Suppression) reconcile duplicate candidate detections into clean, final predictions.\n* **Data Augmentation**: Geometric transformations expose models to diverse spatial viewpoints, significantly improving generalization to unseen test images.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: image = [[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]], pad = 1, crop_h = 2, crop_w = 2\n\nOutput: [[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated cv_zero_pad_and_center_crop under standard test conditions."
